-- title:
--   Descent of Hilbert-functor points from geometric fibres to the base
-- statement:
--   Fix $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and $P \in \mathbb{Q}[t]$, and assume `hP`: there is a field $K$ and an ideal $I \subseteq K[x_0,\dots,x_n]$ stable under taking homogeneous components, together with $d_1$, such that for all $d \ge d_1$ the $K$-dimension of the degree-$d$ forms modulo $I$ equals $P(d)$. The conclusion asserts the existence of $D_0 \in \mathbb{N}$ such that for every $m \ge D_0$ the following holds for every commutative ring $A$, every scheme $Z$ and every morphism $\iota : Z \to \mathbb{P}^n_A = \operatorname{Proj}$ of the graded ring of forms in $n+1$ variables over $A$: if $\iota$ is a closed immersion and the composite of $\iota$ with the structure map $\mathbb{P}^n_A \to \operatorname{Spec} A$ is flat and locally of finite presentation, and if for every algebraically closed field $k$ which is an $A$-algebra and every closed immersion $\iota_k : Z_k \to \mathbb{P}^n_k$ together with $e : Z_k \to Z$ making $Z_k$ the base change of $Z$ along $\operatorname{Spec} k \to \operatorname{Spec} A$ (as a pullback square of the two structure morphisms) and satisfying $\iota \circ e = \operatorname{map}(A,k) \circ \iota_k$, there exists a point $q$ of the Hilbert functor over $k$ for the function $d \mapsto \binom{n+d}{n}$ for $d < m$ and $d \mapsto \lfloor P(d)\rfloor$ for $d \ge m$ — that is, an ideal $q.I$ of $k[x_0,\dots,x_n]$ stable under homogeneous components whose degree-$d$ quotients are finite projective of that rank at every prime — with the property that for all $d \ge m$ a form $F$ of degree $d$ lies in $q.I$ exactly when, for each $i$, the pullback under $\iota_k$ of the degree-zero homogeneous localisation class $F/x_i^d$ on the basic open set $D_+(x_i)$ vanishes; then there exists a point $p$ of the Hilbert functor over $A$ for the same function, whose ideal $p.I$ is precisely the ideal generated by those forms $F \in A[x_0,\dots,x_n]$ that are homogeneous of some degree $d \ge m$ and satisfy $\iota^\sharp(F/x_i^d) = 0$ on $D_+(x_i)$ for every $i$.
--
--   This is the cohomology-and-base-change step in the construction of the Hilbert scheme: for a closed, flat, locally finitely presented family in $\mathbb{P}^n_A$, the truncated ideal of forms of degree $\ge m$ vanishing on $Z$ defines a point of the Hilbert functor over $A$ once the corresponding statement is known on all geometric fibres. It is used in the representability results for the Hilbert functor and in the construction of embedded moduli of framed polarised abelian schemes.
